.TH CLRU_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clru_declare_scoped \- declare an LRU cache that is destroyed at scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

clru_declare_scoped(name, KeyT, ValT);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The same as clru_declare(), plus
__attribute__((cleanup(___clrucache_destroy))) on the variable, so the
compiler destroys the cache when the variable leaves its scope. Call
clru_init() on the variable before you use the cache.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  Variable name for the cache handle
KeyT  Key type
ValT  Value type
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro needs GCC or Clang, because it uses __attribute__((cleanup)).

An array key type or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], is a
compile error, and the message says to wrap the array in a struct. The
macros copy a key or a value by assignment, C cannot assign an array,
and without the error the cache would store the address of the
caller's array instead of its elements. A struct that holds the
array works.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_declare (3),
.BR clru_construct_scoped (3)
